  "story": {
    "headline": "How to Watch the Total Solar Eclipse on a Live Stream, Witnessing the Rare View of the Sun's Corona From Your Own Home",
    "summary": "Wednesday’s eclipse will bring dark skies to parts of Russia, Greenland, Iceland, Portugal and Spain. But with these online broadcasts, you can view the spectacle from anywhere",
    "publisher": "Rally News",
    "url": "https://rally.news/categories/entertainment/?article=How%2Bto%2BWatch%2Bthe%2BTotal%2BSolar%2BEclipse%2Bon%2Ba%2BLive%2BStream%2C%2BWitnessing%2Bthe%2BRare%2BView%2Bof%2Bthe%2BSun%27s%2BCorona%2BFrom%2BYour%2BOwn%2BHome"
  },
  "researchBrief": "The August 12th solar eclipse will be visible in parts of Europe and North America, with totality passing over Greenland, Iceland, Spain, and a corner of Portugal. Multiple organizations, including NASA, the European Space Agency (ESA), and the Virtual Telescope Project, are offering live streams of the event. NASA’s broadcast will feature interviews with experts and live telescope views, while ESA will stream from the Astrophysical Observatory of Javalambre in Spain. The Virtual Telescope Project will cover the eclipse from both Italy and Spain. All sources corroborate the availability of these livestreams and the path of totality. The key numbers, such as the time of totality and the percentage of the sun covered in different regions, are consistent across sources.",
  "sources": [
